<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n2352" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">amplĭfĭcātĭo</span></b> ,
			ōnis, f.
		amplifico, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a widening;</i> hence, </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>An extending</i>, <i>enlarging</i>,
				<i>increasing</i> (perh. only in Cic.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">pecuniae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Div. 2, 14, 33</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">rei familiaris,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					Off. 1, 8, 25</span></i>
			.—<b>Trop.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">honoris et gloriae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Off. 2, 12, 42</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> In
			rhet. t. t., <i>an exaggerated description of an
			object</i>, <i>an amplification</i>, Auct. ad
					<i><span class="en">Her. 2, 30</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Cic.
				Part. Or. 15</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Quint. 2, 5, 9</span></i>; <i><span class="en">5, 10, 99</span></i>; v.
			amplifico, II.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
